<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sébastien Rale</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1657">1657</a>–<a href="/years/1724">1724</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Sébastien Rasle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sébastien Rale (1657–1724) was a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary who worked among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abenaki">Abenaki</a> of the Wabanaki Confederacy from the 1690s, based for decades at the village of Norridgewock on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kennebec-river">Kennebec River</a> in present-day Maine. He compiled a dictionary of the Abenaki language and encouraged resistance to English settlement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> militia killed him in a raid on Norridgewock in 1724, during the conflict known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dummers-war">Father Rale's War</a> or Dummer's War.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sebastien-rale.jpg" alt="Sébastien Rale" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas W. Strong, lithograph publisher, 98 Nassau Street, New York · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Death_of_Father_Sebastian_Rale_of_the_Society_of_Jesus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
